\name{BaseRSurvfitLayerProcessor}
\alias{BaseRSurvfitLayerProcessor}
\title{Base R Kaplan-Meier Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads \code{plot()} of a \code{survival::survfit} object -- a Kaplan-Meier curve,
one per stratum -- from the object itself, as a \code{survival} layer.
\subsection{Why the object and not the drawing}{

\code{survival:::plot.survfit()} draws from inside the survival namespace: a
\code{plot(type = "n")} for the frame, then one \code{lines()} per curve of a
staircase it builds itself, \code{points()} for the censoring marks and two
more \code{lines()} for the confidence band (survival 3.5). Those resolve
through survival's imports of graphics, so maidr's wrappers see none of
them; what is recorded is the one \code{plot(fit)} call the reader wrote, which
typed by \code{type} alone was an empty scatter.
}

\subsection{The reading}{

One series per stratum, each point a time the object reports: the
survival estimate there, \code{censored} where a subject left the study at
that time without the event (\code{n.censor}), and the band's \code{yMin} and
\code{yMax} when the plot draws it. The curve starts where the plot starts it,
at \code{survival::survfit0()}'s added origin. Times are divided by \code{xscale}
and estimates multiplied by \code{yscale}, which is how the plot's axes label
them; a curve cut at \code{xmax} (or at the right end of \code{xlim}) is cut as the
plot cuts it.

Censoring is read whether or not \code{mark.time} draws the marks: it is part
of the estimate the curve is drawn from, not of its decoration, and it is
what separates a flat tail many subjects support from one few do.

Highlight: gridGraphics names each \code{lines()} polyline
\code{graphics-plot-N-lines-M} in drawing order -- the curve, then its lower
and upper bound when the band is drawn -- so each stratum's curve is the
first of its three, or every one without a band.

See \code{\link[=survfit_layer_type]{survfit_layer_type()}} for the drawings that are declined.
}
}
